findings.body:
The threshold loop changed from `<=` to `<`, and the high-volume branch fires at `>= 400_000`. So a `lastCount` that lands exactly on a tier limit gets the *next* tier's multiplier. That contradicts the doc table ("101 – 1k → 1.25×", "100k – 400k → 2×") and this PR's own tests:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="124-135"}

I ran the new logic against the progression table in the unit test:

| lastCount | test expects | code returns |
|---|---|---|
| 1,000 | 1,250 | **1,500** |
| 5,000 | 7,500 | **8,750** |
| 100,000 | 175,000 | **200,000** |
| 400,000 | 800,000 | **475,000** |

::code{file="ghost/core/test/unit/server/services/email-service/domain-warming-service.test.ts" lines="201-207"}

`should return calculated limit when emailCount is greater` (line 127, expects 1250 for lastCount=1000) fails too. The integration test's `getExpectedScale` helper also uses `<=` boundaries, so the whole PR assumes inclusive upper limits. Only the implementation disagrees.

Fix: make the limits inclusive and start the high-volume branch strictly above 400k.

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="124-135"}
        // For high volume senders (400k+), cap the increase at 20% or 75k absolute
        if (lastCount > WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.threshold) {
            const scaledIncrease = Math.ceil(lastCount * WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxScale);
            const absoluteIncrease = lastCount + WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxAbsoluteIncrease;
            return Math.min(scaledIncrease, absoluteIncrease);
        }

        for (const threshold of WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.thresholds.sort((a, b) => a.limit - b.limit)) {
            if (lastCount <= threshold.limit) {
                return Math.ceil(lastCount * threshold.scale);
            }
        }

Moving to `findPage` also changed the filter operator from `<` to `<=`:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="97-105"}

The docstring right above still says "excluding today". The warmup model depends on not compounding within a day: a second send on the same day shouldn't scale off the first one. Depending on how the DB compares a datetime to a bare date (`2026-11-23` turns into midnight in MySQL, or a plain string compare in SQLite), `<=` either does almost nothing or pulls today's sends into the max. Either way it's an unintended semantic change.

It also breaks the PR's own unit test. `"created_at:<=2026-11-23".includes("created_at:<2026-11-23")` is `false`:

::code{file="ghost/core/test/unit/server/services/email-service/domain-warming-service.test.ts" lines="181-181"}

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="102-102"}
            filter: `created_at:<${new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0]}`,

`highVolume.threshold` and the last `thresholds[].limit` both hard-code 400_000. The fallback after the loop is only correct because those two numbers stay in sync. If someone changes one, say raising the last threshold to 500k, the fallback quietly applies an uncapped 1.2× with no +75k limit.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="136-138"}

You could derive the high-volume threshold from the last tier, or have the fallback call the same `min(1.2×, +75k)` logic so it's correct no matter what. `thresholds.sort()` also mutates the module-level constant on every call. It's harmless, but sorting once at definition time would be cleaner.
